#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dt-install-drift — is every file B-CREDENTIALS-PRIVATE-REPO installed on THIS box byte-for-byte
the committed copy? Pre-audit §2.2 item 10 (C-18). Runs as root, hourly, on each box; a failure
pages through the box's failure unit (staging: dt-unit-failure@ → a system alert naming Infra
Claude; Helsinki: agent-unit-failure@ → the crew channel).

⛔ ROOT RUNS NO GIT. The committed copy's blob id is read by the account that OWNS the repo
   (deploy on staging, langston on Helsinki), and root re-hashes the installed file itself
   (sha1 of "blob <len>\\0<bytes>", git's own object hash). A deploy-owned repo read by root
   would hand root that repo's config — the class safe.directory exists to prevent.
⚠️ STATED RESIDUAL: the blob id comes from the repo owner's git, so an owner who could edit both
   the repo and an installed file could make them agree. On staging that owner is deploy, who is
   root until #1102 — so this detects accidents and hand edits, it is not a tamper boundary.

A file it cannot read is a FAIL, never a skip. Every line it prints is one fact:
    OK       <installed path>  = <repo path> at <sha>
    DRIFT    <installed path>  differs from <repo path> at <sha>
    MISSING  <installed path>
    PERM     <installed path>  mode/owner is X, wanted Y
    STATE    a runtime fact that must hold (dt-api's state, a page, the mint key's pairing)
    SOURCE   the committed source itself is unreadable or stale — a mirror-health fact, not drift
EXIT: 0 all OK · 1 anything else (the unit fails, the page fires).
"""
import grp
import hashlib
import json
import os
import pwd
import socket
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# ── CONSTANTS (a test harness substitutes ONLY this block) ──
